<h2>5 U-Shape Variations</h2>
<p>The U-shape adapts to different room shapes and family needs. Here are the five configurations worth knowing.</p>
<h3>1. The Classic Closed U</h3>
<p>Three full walls of cabinetry, open only at the entry. Maximum storage and counter. Best for dedicated kitchens that are not visible from living areas. The most "professional kitchen" feel.</p>
<h3>2. The U with Peninsula</h3>
<p>One arm of the U extends into the room as a peninsula, often with a breakfast bar on the outer edge. This softens the enclosure, adds casual seating, and lets the cook face outward. The best of U and open-plan.</p>
<h3>3. The Broken U (with window)</h3>
<p>When one wall has a window, the U breaks there — the sink usually sits under the window, with counter flanking it. The upper cabinets skip the window wall. Brings natural light deep into the kitchen.</p>
<h3>4. The Tall-Wall U</h3>
<p>One entire wall of the U becomes floor-to-ceiling tall units: pantry, built-in oven, microwave, and fridge in a single seamless column. The other two walls handle wet and hot zones. Unbeatable for storage-obsessed cooks.</p>
<h3>5. The Compact U (small-space)</h3>
<p>A tighter U for kitchens right at the 80 sq.ft. threshold. Uses 5 ft arms, handle-less slab doors, and light colours to avoid the boxed-in feeling. Possible, but an L-shape is often smarter at this size.</p>
