<h2>Ventilation &amp; Light: The U-Shape's Weak Spot</h2>
<p>Because the U-shape encloses the cook on three sides, it traps heat, steam, and cooking odours more than open layouts. In Indian kitchens — where deep frying, tadka, and high-flame cooking are daily events — ventilation is not optional. It is the difference between a comfortable kitchen and a sauna.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Oversize the chimney:</strong> Go one size up from what the hob technically needs. A 90 cm chimney on a 4-burner hob (instead of 60 cm) clears fumes faster in an enclosed U. Aim for suction of 1200+ m³/hr.</li>
  <li><strong>Add cross-ventilation:</strong> If the U has a window, keep it operable. If not, an exhaust fan on the hot wall, diagonally opposite the chimney, creates airflow that pulls heat out.</li>
  <li><strong>Mind the third wall's lighting:</strong> Three walls of upper cabinets cast shadows. Under-cabinet LED strips on all three runs are essential — without them, the centre of the U becomes a dim cave during the day.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep upper cabinets lighter:</strong> In an enclosed U, dark upper cabinets on three walls press inward. Use light tones up high and save darker tones for the lowers to keep the space breathing.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><span class="callout__title">Watch out</span><strong>Don't skip this:</strong> The single most common regret in U-shape kitchens is inadequate ventilation. An enclosed three-wall kitchen with a weak chimney becomes unbearable during Indian cooking. Budget for a powerful chimney before you budget for fancy finishes.</div>
<h2>Storage Capacity: The U-Shape's Superpower</h2>
<p>This is where the U-shape genuinely wins. Three walls of cabinetry — both upper and lower — give roughly 40% more storage than an equivalent L-shape kitchen. For families who store large quantities of provisions, cookware, and appliances, nothing else comes close.</p>
<h3>Maximising the Three Walls</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Full-height tall units on one wall:</strong> A bank of tall pull-out larders stores a month of provisions in a single column.</li>
  <li><strong>Upper cabinets to the ceiling:</strong> On all three walls, extend uppers fully up. The top shelf holds festival cookware and bulk items.</li>
  <li><strong>Deep pan drawers on the hot wall:</strong> Heavy-duty drawers (instead of shutters) under the hob store pots, pans, and pressure cookers with full visibility and easy access.</li>
  <li><strong>Built-in appliance garage:</strong> A roll-shutter cabinet on the prep wall hides the mixer, toaster, and kettle, keeping the counter clear while the appliances stay plugged in and ready.</li>
  <li><strong>Plinth drawers:</strong> The toe-kick space under the lower cabinets becomes shallow drawers for flat items — baking trays, chopping boards, table mats. Free storage most kitchens ignore.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Palettes That Suit an Enclosed U</h2>
<p>Because the U wraps three walls around you, colour behaves differently than in an open L-shape. The enclosure amplifies whatever tone you choose — so the rule here is "go lighter than you think." Four palettes that work specifically because they keep an enclosed kitchen feeling open:</p>
