<p>Expect a U-shape to run roughly <strong>25-40% more than an equivalent L-shape</strong>, driven mostly by the extra wall of cabinetry and the second corner solution. The trade-off is significantly more storage and counter — for a serious cook, often worth every rupee.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>Note:</strong> Costs exclude appliances (chimney, hob, sink, oven) and any electrical/plumbing modifications. A powerful chimney — essential in an enclosed U — adds ₹15,000-45,000.</div>
<h2>Final Verdict: Is the U-Shape Your Kitchen?</h2>
<p>The U-shape is not the "best" kitchen layout — there is no such thing. It is the best layout for a <strong>specific situation</strong>: a roomy kitchen, a serious solo cook, and storage as the top priority. Get those three things right and nothing outperforms it.</p>
<p>If your kitchen is a typical apartment size, if you value open-plan connection, or if two people cook together, the U-shape's enclosure will work against you — and an L-shape or island will serve you better. Match the layout to how you actually live and cook, not to what looks impressive in a showroom.</p>
<p>That is the whole philosophy: <strong>shape before colour, workflow before finish.</strong> Decide the layout based on your space and your cooking, then make it beautiful. Do it in that order and you will have a kitchen that works as well as it looks — for the next fifteen years.</p>
